h2 { "How to read the reference" }

Each entry gives the call signature, accepted inputs, result, execution location and possible failures. Multiple signatures under one name describe different accepted forms of the same call.

T and U stand for supported element types; F stands for a floating type. M, K and N stand for tensor dimensions. A signature using these letters explains a family of calls; it is not a generic declaration to paste into your program. For example, T in map may be i64 when mapping an [i64] array.

Inputs described as completed values are ordinary values, not pending tasks; await a task before passing its result. A resident GPU tensor must be used on its device or transferred to the CPU before a host-only operation.

Type and statically known shape errors are reported before execution. Bounds, dynamic shape, arithmetic and allocation failures are checked while the program runs. An ordinary call does not automatically expose every fault as an application error. Tasks report execution failures through TaskError.

Built-in names are not first-class function values. To use one as a callback, wrap its call in a user-defined function.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/language-reference", "Types, constructors, operators and error syntax" },
